Prompt: Agentic Self-Healing API Test Framework (Java/Maven)
Use this as a single end-to-end prompt for a coding agent (Claude Code, Copilot Workspace, etc.) to scaffold the implementation. Feed it phase by phase if the agent struggles with the whole thing at once — each phase is self-contained.
Context (paste this first, always)
I have an existing Java, Maven-based API test automation framework using Java's built-in HttpClient. I want to add an agentic AI layer on top of it that does two things:
Self-healing on failure: when a test fails, automatically read the test's log/report, classify the failure, and if it's a fixable code issue (bad assertion, stale locator/path, minor logic bug), generate a patch, verify it in isolation, and open a PR — never auto-merge without a passing re-run and human review.
API version-change detection: periodically fetch the OpenAPI/Swagger spec, diff it against the last known version, map changed endpoints to affected tests, and re-run + auto-heal just those tests.
Tech constraints: Java 17+, Maven, JUnit 5 (or TestNG — tell me which you use if it matters), Allure for reporting, Anthropic Claude API for the LLM calls, GitHub for PRs, Jenkins for orchestration. Prefer LangChain4j for agent/tool orchestration over building a custom loop from scratch, since it keeps everything in the Java stack.
Do not aim for full autonomy on day one. Every phase must include a verification/guardrail step before anything gets merged automatically.
Phase 1 — Structured failure capture
Set up Allure (or upgrade existing reporting) so every test run emits a JSON result per test containing: request (method, URL, headers, body), response (status, headers, body), the specific assertion that failed with expected vs actual, full stack trace, and a hash/version tag of the API spec used at run time. Write a small Java utility class TestResultCapture that reads Allure's result JSON files after a run and normalizes them into a single FailureRecord POJO per failed test.
Phase 2 — Failure classifier (rule-based first)
Build a FailureClassifier class that inspects each FailureRecord and buckets it before touching any LLM:
Connection refused / timeout → INFRA_ISSUE (no AI, just flag)
401/403 → AUTH_ISSUE (no AI, just flag)
5xx response → SERVER_SIDE (no AI, just flag)
Assertion mismatch where the field also appears in a recent spec diff → CONTRACT_DRIFT (send to AI with spec diff context)
Assertion mismatch with no spec change → AMBIGUOUS_ASSERTION (send to AI)
Stack trace pointing to a locator/path/field reference error → CODE_BUG (send to AI)
Only CONTRACT_DRIFT, AMBIGUOUS_ASSERTION, and CODE_BUG go to Phase 3.
Phase 3 — LLM diagnosis + patch generation (LangChain4j)
Using LangChain4j, define an agent with these tools:
readTestSource(testClassName) → returns the Java source of the failing test
readFailureRecord(testId) → returns the structured failure JSON from Phase 1
readApiSpecDiff() → returns the latest OpenAPI diff, if any (see Phase 5)
proposePatch(testClassName, unifiedDiff) → the agent's output tool call, must return a valid unified diff, not prose
System prompt for the agent: it must (a) explain its diagnosis in one paragraph, (b) output a minimal unified diff touching only the failing test method, (c) never weaken or delete an assertion just to make a test pass without flagging that explicitly as a red flag in its explanation.
Phase 4 — Sandbox verification + PR automation
Build a PatchVerifier service:
Create a new git branch off the current commit (JGit).
Apply the LLM's diff to that branch only (java-diff-utils to parse/apply).
Run only the affected test via ProcessBuilder (mvn test -Dtest=ClassName#method) in that branch's working directory.
If it passes: open a GitHub PR (via github-api library) with the diff, the original failure log, and the LLM's diagnosis paragraph in the PR description.
If it fails: discard the branch, tag the failure record as AUTO_FIX_FAILED, and leave it for manual triage. Do not retry more than once.
Phase 5 — API spec change detection
Build a scheduled job (quartz, or a Jenkins cron stage) that:
Fetches the current OpenAPI/Swagger spec.
Diffs it against the last stored copy using openapi-diff-core.
If differences exist, maps changed endpoints to test classes using a manifest file you maintain (endpoint -> [test class#method]).
Triggers a re-run of just the mapped tests, feeding the diff into Phase 3's readApiSpecDiff() tool.
Stores the new spec as the "last known" version once processed.
Phase 6 — Jenkins orchestration
Add a post-test-stage in the Jenkins pipeline: after mvn test runs, invoke the healing service (either as a Maven exec goal or a small Spring Boot REST endpoint) passing the Allure results directory. The stage should be non-blocking — a failed auto-heal attempt should not fail the build, just log and notify (Slack/email) that manual triage is needed.
Non-negotiable guardrails (repeat in every phase)
No auto-fix is ever merged to main without a human-approved PR, at least for the first several weeks of operation.
No patch touches more than the single failing test method's diff.
Any patch that changes or removes an assertion must be flagged prominently, never silently accepted.
Track metrics: how many auto-generated patches were accepted vs rejected on review, to decide later whether to relax the human-approval gate for specific failure categories.